# Slate look

Match the supplied TickTick screenshots. Keep Slate branding and system icons.

- Task lists are a large title and one rounded card of checkbox rows. Dates sit on the right in blue (`Today`, `Tomorrow`, `Oct 19`). Do not put a search field in the list.
- Adding is a blue circle at the bottom right. On iPhone it opens `QuickAddComposer`: a compact card sitting on the keyboard over a dimmed screen. It is never a full-screen or detent sheet. It has a title line (`What would you like to do?`), a description line, and an icon row (calendar, list, priority, tag), with a blue up-arrow send button on the right. Do not leave an add field at the top of the list.
- Natural-language words in the title (`tmrw`, `5pm`, `#tag`, `p1`) are highlighted blue inline. The calendar icon turns blue and shows the parsed day (`Tomorrow`).
- The iPhone app uses the native SwiftUI `TabView` with four `Tab` items: Tasks, Calendar, Habits, Countdown. There is no More tab. Do not replace it with a custom drawn dock. The mobile website keeps its floating capsule dock.
- The list sidebar is Today, Tomorrow, Next 7 Days, then lists with counts, Archived Lists, Completed, and Trash. The selected row is a dark rounded pill.
- Date carousels (habits, calendar day view) always start on Sunday and page one full week per swipe. Do not scroll day by day. Past dates are tappable, and check-ins apply to the selected day. iOS uses the shared `WeekPager`.
- Every hamburger button opens the lists menu as a left slide-in drawer over a dimmed screen (tap outside or swipe left to close). Picking a list closes it and shows Tasks. Do not use a tab or a sheet for it.
- Calendar must let you complete tasks (checkbox in the day and list views, context menu in month) and add tasks (blue + dated to the selected day).
- The app reopens where it was left: tab, task list or smart view, and calendar view mode are kept in `@AppStorage`. The selected calendar day is kept within the same day and resets to today on a new day.
- Pulling down on the iPhone calendar's week strip (or overscrolling the Today list, or tapping the grabber) expands it into a full month grid. Swipe sideways to change months, tap a day to select it and collapse back to that week. Pushing up or scrolling the list collapses it.
- iOS 26 toolbar items already get a Liquid Glass circle. Toolbar button labels are bare SF Symbols with no `.background`, `.frame`, or custom circle (that draws a second outline). Custom headers outside a toolbar use `.glassEffect(.regular.interactive(), in: Circle()/Capsule())` to match.
- Google Calendar events are cached per day on disk (iOS `google.json`, web `localStorage`) and shown immediately, then refreshed in the background when older than 2 minutes. Never block the calendar on a Google status or events request, and never overwrite the cache with an empty list from a failed request.
- The app opens instantly from the on-device cache. `ready` and `unlocked` are set before any network call, and sync runs afterward.
- On iPhone, and on the website at phone width, Calendar opens as a scrollable week strip, then a Today card and a Habit card. Month is the separate view from the calendar button, and that view still pinches to zoom and scrolls. The website keeps the other calendar modes behind the same menu, and a phone-width lists button opens the list drawer.
- The iPhone calendar header has a round hamburger on the left, the month name over `Today` in the center, and one capsule on the right holding a view icon and an ellipsis. The week strip shows `W42` under Sunday and gray dots under busy days. Today-card rows have a colored left bar (list or event color), a `G` badge for Google events or a square checkbox for tasks, a bold title, a blue time or `Today` on the right, and a repeat icon under it for repeating tasks.
- Settings lists each Google calendar beside sign-in, with the same show and hide toggles as the website. A hidden calendar stays off the calendar on that device.
- Calendar month view scrolls vertically through months and still pinches to zoom. Pinch changes how many weeks fit. One-finger or trackpad scroll keeps moving through months. Do not let either gesture turn the other off.
- Countdowns are dense rows: icon or image, truncated title, and a large day count that stays visible (`72` / `Days`).
